CAT = "corrReading"; SEG = "RDG"
# Understanding and interpreting written material. Each passage is original and describes a
# fictional facility or general idea; answers come only from what the passage says.
Q = [
("Read the passage: Direct supervision puts an officer inside the housing unit with the inmates instead of behind a glass control booth. The officer moves around, talks with inmates, and notices tension early. Supporters say problems are handled while they are small, before they turn into fights. What advantage of direct supervision does the passage give?",
 "Problems are caught while small", ["Fewer officers are needed per unit", "Inmates spend more time in cells", "Booths become easier to staff"],
 "The passage says the officer notices tension early so problems are handled while small. Staffing levels and cell time are not mentioned."),
("Read the passage: At intake, each new arrival is asked about medical conditions, current medications and thoughts of self-harm. The answers go to the nurse the same day. Arrivals who report thoughts of self-harm are placed on watch until a mental health worker sees them. According to the passage, who decides when the watch can end?",
 "It does not say", ["The intake officer", "The nurse on duty", "The shift sergeant"],
 "The passage says arrivals stay on watch until a mental health worker sees them, but it never says who ends the watch. Choosing a person goes beyond the text."),
("Read the passage: Contraband enters facilities in many ways: thrown over fences, hidden in mail, passed during visits, or brought in by staff. Because the routes are so varied, no single search method stops all of it. Facilities combine mail screening, visit supervision, perimeter patrols and staff searches. What is the main point of the passage?",
 "Several methods are needed together", ["Staff bring in most contraband", "Mail screening is the best method", "Fences should be built higher"],
 "The passage lists many routes and concludes that methods must be combined. It never ranks the routes or the methods."),
("Read the passage: Officers who write clear reports save time later. A supervisor who can understand a report on the first reading does not need to call the officer back to explain it. Clear reports also hold up better when they are read months later in a hearing. Which benefit of clear reports does the passage NOT mention?",
 "Faster promotion for the officer", ["Fewer follow-up questions", "Better use in a later hearing", "Saving time later"],
 "Saving time, fewer calls back and use in a hearing are all in the passage. Promotion is never mentioned."),
("Read the passage: In the unit's earned privilege system, inmates start at Level 1. After 60 days without a rule violation, they move to Level 2, which adds evening dayroom time. After 60 more days without a violation, they reach Level 3, which adds a second weekly phone call. An inmate with 90 days and no violations is at which level?",
 "Level 2", ["Level 1", "Level 3", "Between levels"],
 "Level 2 starts at 60 days and Level 3 at 120 days. At 90 days the inmate is at Level 2."),
("Read the passage: Many officers find the first year the hardest. Shift work disturbs sleep, and the job's stress follows officers home. Officers who keep regular exercise, talk with peers and use the employee assistance program report coping better. According to the passage, which group reports coping better?",
 "Those using the support offered", ["Officers who work only day shifts", "Officers with prior military service", "Officers who work overtime"],
 "The passage links coping to exercise, peer talk and the assistance program. Shift type, military service and overtime are not discussed."),
("Read the passage: A good post order tells the officer what to do, when, and how. It does not repeat the whole policy manual; it points to it. Post orders are reviewed every year and whenever the post changes, so that officers are never working from outdated steps. Why are post orders reviewed when a post changes?",
 "So the steps stay current", ["So the manual can be shorter", "So officers can choose their posts", "So reviews happen only yearly"],
 "The passage says reviews keep officers from working from outdated steps. A changed post makes old steps outdated."),
("Read the passage: Unit 4 has 64 beds. On Monday 58 were filled, and on Tuesday 3 inmates were released while 5 were admitted. The unit is considered crowded when more than 60 beds are filled. Based on the passage, was the unit crowded on Tuesday?",
 "No, with 60 filled", ["Yes, with 62 filled", "No, with 56 filled", "Yes, with 66 filled"],
 "58 minus 3 plus 5 is 60. The unit is crowded only when more than 60 beds are filled, so with exactly 60 it was not crowded."),
("Read the passage: Inmates may file a grievance about conditions or staff conduct. The grievance goes first to the unit manager, who answers within 10 working days. An inmate who disagrees may appeal to the warden within 5 working days of the answer. What happens if the unit manager does not answer?",
 "The passage does not say", ["The inmate appeals to the warden", "The grievance is granted", "The grievance is closed"],
 "The passage gives the answer deadline but not what happens when it is missed. Any of the other choices goes beyond the text."),
("Read the passage: Officers are taught to use the least force needed to gain control. Spoken commands come first. If they fail, officers may use physical control holds. Chemical agents are used only when holds would put officers or inmates at greater risk of injury. When may chemical agents be used, according to the passage?",
 "If holds would risk more injury", ["Whenever spoken commands fail", "Whenever an inmate is armed", "Before spoken commands are given"],
 "The passage limits chemical agents to cases where holds would raise the risk of injury. Failed commands lead first to holds, not agents."),
("Read the passage: The visiting room has two officers. One checks visitors in at the desk and the other watches the tables. If the table officer must leave the room, the desk officer stops check-in until the table officer returns. Why does check-in stop?",
 "So the tables are not left unwatched", ["So visitors can be searched again", "So the desk officer can take a break", "So visits can end early"],
 "With the table officer gone, the desk officer must watch the tables, so check-in pauses. The passage gives no other reason."),
("Read the passage: Most violent incidents in the jail happen in the first 48 hours after arrival and in the hour before shift change. Supervisors have added an officer to the intake unit and moved staff briefings so they do not leave units short at shift change. What problem were the changes meant to address?",
 "Violence at two high-risk times", ["Slow processing at intake", "Long staff briefings", "Too few officers overall"],
 "Both changes target the two times the passage names: the first 48 hours and the hour before shift change."),
("Read the passage: Inmates who take part in education programs are less likely to return to prison after release, studies have found. The benefit is largest for programs that lead to a credential, such as a high school equivalency diploma or a trade certificate. Which program would the passage suggest helps most?",
 "One that ends in a trade certificate", ["One with the most class hours", "One taught by outside teachers", "One that inmates choose freely"],
 "The passage says the benefit is largest when a program leads to a credential, such as a trade certificate."),
("Read the passage: A shakedown is a thorough search of a cell or area. Officers search from top to bottom and from one side to the other so nothing is skipped. Each item is put back as it was found unless it is contraband. What does the passage say about items that are not contraband?",
 "They go back as found", ["They are logged and kept", "They are returned the next day", "They are moved to storage"],
 "The passage says non-contraband items are put back as they were found."),
("Read the passage: Escorting officers walk behind and slightly to one side of the inmate, not in front. From that spot they can see the inmate's hands and react if he turns. Two officers are used for inmates with a recent assault charge. Why does the officer walk behind and to one side?",
 "To keep the hands in view", ["To see the hallway ahead", "To stay out of the way of staff", "To let the inmate set the pace"],
 "The passage says that spot lets the officer see the inmate's hands and react if he turns."),
("Read the passage: In the reentry unit, inmates within six months of release attend job readiness classes, open a bank account and get a state ID card before they leave. Staff say the ID card matters most, because without it a person cannot rent housing or start most jobs. Why do staff say the ID card matters most?",
 "It is needed for housing and work", ["It is the cheapest step", "It takes the longest to get", "It is required for classes"],
 "The passage gives the reason: without the ID card a person cannot rent housing or start most jobs."),
("Read the passage: Officers must report any sexual abuse they see, hear about, or suspect, even if the inmate asks them to keep it secret. The report goes to the shift commander, who starts the facility's response. An inmate tells an officer about abuse and asks him to tell no one. What does the passage require?",
 "Report it to the shift commander", ["Keep it secret as asked", "Report it only if it happens again", "Ask the inmate to file it himself"],
 "The passage requires a report even when the inmate asks for secrecy, and it names the shift commander."),
("Read the passage: The jail switched from paper count sheets to handheld scanners that read each inmate's wristband. Counts now take about 12 minutes instead of 25, and fewer errors are found later. However, the scanners need charging, and a dead scanner forces a paper count. What drawback of the scanners does the passage mention?",
 "They must be kept charged", ["They are hard to learn", "They cost more than paper", "They miss some wristbands"],
 "The only drawback given is charging: a dead scanner forces a paper count."),
("Read the passage: An officer's log is a running record of everything that happens on post. Entries are made in ink, in time order, and never erased. A mistake is crossed out with one line and initialed, so the original can still be read. Why is a mistake crossed out with one line?",
 "So the original stays readable", ["So the log looks neat", "So entries stay in time order", "So the sergeant can find it fast"],
 "The passage says a single line keeps the original readable."),
("Read the passage: In hot weather, the jail opens extra cooling areas, gives out more water, and checks on inmates who take medications that make heat illness more likely. Officers are trained to look for confusion, heavy sweating or no sweating at all. Which inmates get extra checks, according to the passage?",
 "Those on certain medications", ["Those in top-tier cells", "Those over age 60", "Those who ask for water"],
 "The passage names inmates whose medications raise the risk of heat illness. Age and cell location are not mentioned."),
("Read the passage: Rumors spread quickly in a housing unit and can start fights. Officers who hear a rumor about a planned fight should tell a supervisor, even if it seems unlikely. A rumor that turns out false costs little; one that is ignored can cost a life. What does the author mean by \"costs little\"?",
 "Checking a false one does little harm", ["Rumors are cheap and easy to start in a unit", "False rumors are seldom reported", "Officers seldom hear rumors"],
 "The author compares the small cost of checking a false rumor with the large cost of ignoring a true one."),
("Read the passage: Food trays are counted out of the kitchen and counted back in. A missing tray is treated as missing contraband, because tray parts can be made into weapons. The unit is not unlocked for the next activity until all trays are back. Why is a missing tray treated seriously?",
 "Its parts can become weapons", ["Trays are costly to replace", "The kitchen needs it back fast", "It delays the next meal"],
 "The passage says tray parts can be made into weapons, so a missing tray is handled like missing contraband."),
("Read the passage: The facility uses three colors of wristband: white for general population, yellow for medical watch and red for inmates who must be kept apart from others. An officer escorting a red-band inmate sees a white-band inmate in the hallway. Based on the passage, what must the officer keep in mind?",
 "Red-band inmates must be kept apart", ["White-band inmates need a medical check", "Yellow bands outrank red bands", "Escorts end in shared hallways"],
 "Red bands mark inmates who must be kept apart from others, so the officer must keep the two separated."),
("Read the passage: Some jails let families send video messages instead of letters. Messages are screened before inmates see them, just as letters are. Officials say the main benefit is that paper, which can be soaked with drugs, no longer comes in the mail. What is the main benefit, according to officials?",
 "Less drug-soaked paper coming in", ["Faster delivery to inmates", "Less staff time screening", "Lower costs for families"],
 "The passage names one main benefit: drug-soaked paper no longer comes in the mail."),
("Read the passage: When an inmate threatens to harm himself, the officer stays with him, removes items he could use, and calls for medical and mental health staff. The officer does not leave to get help; help comes to the officer. What should the officer NOT do?",
 "Leave to find help", ["Stay with the inmate", "Remove items he could use", "Call medical staff"],
 "The passage says the officer does not leave; help comes to the officer. The other actions are steps the passage requires."),
("Read the passage: Inmates on work release leave each morning for jobs in the community and must be back by 1900. One who returns after 1900 without calling ahead is treated as an escapee until he is found. An inmate calls at 1830 to say his bus is late and arrives at 1915. Under the passage, is he treated as an escapee?",
 "No, he called ahead", ["Yes, he arrived after 1900", "Yes, buses are no excuse", "No, he was only 15 minutes late"],
 "The escape rule covers late returns without calling ahead. He called at 1830, so he is not treated as an escapee. The 15 minutes is not the reason."),
("Read the passage: Officers learn more by watching than by listening. An inmate may say he is fine while his hands shake and he avoids eye contact. Trained officers note such signs and ask follow-up questions instead of accepting the first answer. Which statement best sums up the passage?",
 "Behavior can say more than words", ["Inmates seldom tell the truth", "Questions upset most inmates", "Eye contact shows guilt"],
 "The passage contrasts what an inmate says with what his body shows. It does not claim inmates usually lie or that eye contact shows guilt."),
("Read the passage: The jail's fire plan names a primary and a backup exit route for every unit. Officers walk both routes during training so they know them in smoke. Routes are posted inside each officer station. Where are the routes posted?",
 "In each officer station", ["In each inmate's cell", "At the facility entrance", "In the training room only"],
 "The passage says the routes are posted inside each officer station."),
("Read the passage: Count time is quiet time. Inmates stand by their bunks, radios and televisions are off, and no one moves until the count clears. Quiet makes the count faster and makes it easier to hear a problem. Which reason for quiet does the passage give?",
 "Problems are easier to hear", ["Inmates can rest", "Officers can make calls", "Lights can be turned off"],
 "The passage gives two reasons: faster counts and hearing problems more easily."),
("Read the passage: Officers rotate between posts every few months. Rotation keeps officers from becoming too familiar with any one group of inmates, which can lead to favors and broken rules. It also builds officers who can work any post. What risk does rotation reduce?",
 "Officers getting too familiar", ["Officers getting bored", "Inmates moving too often", "Posts being left empty"],
 "The passage says rotation keeps officers from becoming too familiar, which can lead to favors and broken rules."),
("Read the passage: A use of force is reviewed by three people: the shift commander, the deputy warden and a training officer. The training officer looks only at whether the techniques matched training, not whether force was justified. What does the training officer review?",
 "If techniques matched training", ["Whether force was justified", "Whether medical care was given", "Whether the report was on time"],
 "The passage limits the training officer to the techniques and says justification is not part of that review."),
("Read the passage: Many jails hold people who have not been convicted and are waiting for trial. Because they are legally innocent, they are usually housed apart from sentenced inmates when space allows. Why does the passage say they are kept apart?",
 "They have not been convicted", ["They are more dangerous", "Their stays are shorter", "They need more programs"],
 "The passage links separate housing to their not having been convicted. It says nothing about danger, stay length or programs."),
]
